#version 460

// ===========================================================================
// WAVEFRONT SCATTER -- THE REPLAY INSTANTIATION
// ===========================================================================
//
// The SECOND instantiation of shaders/includes/diff/traverse.glsl. Its
// sibling shaders/diff/wf_scatter.comp is the first. Everything the scatter
// stage does is in that one include -- extensions, bindings, the Push block,
// the local size, the constants, the RNG reconstruction and fast-forward, the
// BSDF, the environment sample, next-event estimation and MIS, the path
// advance and the re-queue. Neither file adds anything to it except the body
// of `diffVertexHook` and a `main` that calls `diffTraverse()`.
//
// WHAT "REPLAY" MEANS HERE, AND WHAT IT IS NOT.
//
// This stage is dispatched in the SAME position of the SAME
// ohao::diff::WavefrontLoop that the forward stage occupies, in a second,
// independent run of the whole loop -- generate, then [intersect, scatter] x
// bounces -- from re-zeroed buffers with the SAME iteration seed. It is not
// handed the forward run's path, its RNG stream, or any record of what the
// forward run did. It re-derives all of it, which it can only do because the
// seed invariant holds (spec section 4.5): a path is a pure function of
// (pixel, sampleIndex, iterationSeed), every stage reconstructs its RNG from
// (pixelIndex, sampleIndex, iterationSeed) and fast-forwards by the `bounce`
// it reads out of path state, and nothing survives in a register across a
// dispatch boundary.
//
// That is exactly the property path-replay backpropagation rests on, and it
// is why this task exists BEFORE any adjoint does. If the replayed path
// diverges from the forward path by so much as one RNG draw, every gradient
// computed on it is silently wrong -- no crash, no NaN, no diagnostic -- and
// found later it would look like a bad df/dtheta derivation rather than like
// a stream that went out of step. `diff_gpu_probe.cpp`'s replay-equivalence
// check compares this stage's binding-3 vertex trace against the forward
// stage's, bit for bit, for every path and every bounce: the five draws, the
// draw count, and the origin, direction, throughput and hit distance each
// traversal read out of path state.
//
// WHAT THE HOOK DOES, AND WHAT IT DID.
//
// Stage 1 Task 1 left it EMPTY on purpose: the point of that task was to
// establish that two instantiations walk one path, with nothing else in the
// picture to confuse the diagnosis with. Stage 1 Task 2 filled it with the
// first gradient in this project -- d(film)/d(albedo), scattered into the
// gradient arena at binding 10 by one atomicAdd per vertex. The whole of the
// difference between the forward pass and the replay pass is still exactly
// this function's body: the forward one accumulates the film, this one
// accumulates its derivative.
//
// The replay-equivalence claim above is UNCHANGED by that and is the reason
// the hook contract is worth restating here. A hook may write its own sink
// and `v`; it may not write path state, the queues or the counters. This one
// writes one float of the arena and `v.adjoint`. If it wrote anything else,
// check 36 would be the thing that noticed -- one bounce later.
//
// THE RECURSION, FOR THIS INTEGRATOR. Spec section 6.1 writes the scatter
// line for a single-strategy estimator. This integrator's direct-lighting
// estimate is a TWO-STRATEGY MIS combination -- next event and BSDF sampling
// -- so the scatter line is a sum over the strategies and each term carries
// that strategy's own direction, density, radiance, visibility and weight:
//
//     dL/dtheta += v.adjoint *
//                  ( v.wEnv  * (df/dtheta at v.envDir)  * max(dot(v.normal, v.envDir), 0)
//                            * v.envRadiance  * v.visEnv  / v.envPdf
//                  + v.wBsdf * (d(f*cos)/dtheta at v.bsdfDir)
//                            * v.bsdfRadiance * v.visBsdf / v.pdf )
//     v.adjoint  =  v.adjoint * v.bsdfWeight
//
// (In the BSDF term the cosine is already folded into `v.f`, which is f*cos
// at `v.bsdfDir`, so what that term needs is the derivative of `v.f` itself.)
//
// AN EARLIER VERSION OF THIS FILE PRINTED THE SCATTER LINE AS
// `v.adjoint * (df/dtheta) * v.Li / v.pdf`, which was wrong twice over:
// `v.Li` (now `v.Lr`) is the MIS-combined REFLECTED direct radiance, so it
// already contains f and has already been divided by the densities --
// substituting it there double-counts f and squares the pdf -- and a single
// (f, pdf) pair cannot stand for two strategies with two directions and two
// densities anyway. No gradient existed when that line was written, so
// nothing computed a wrong number; what it would have produced is a Task 3
// that read as a bad df/dtheta derivation. The derivation and the field
// names are in `DiffVertex`'s comment in traverse.glsl, which is the
// authority; this is a pointer to it.
//
// The PROPAGATE line above needs no such correction and is exact as written:
// `v.bsdfWeight` is bit-identically the value the traversal multiplied
// throughput by.
//
// AND THE SCATTER LINE, EVEN CORRECTED, IS NOT THE WHOLE DERIVATIVE ONCE THE
// LOOP RUNS MORE THAN ONE BOUNCE. That was found by MEASUREMENT, not by
// re-reading: the finite-difference gate accepts it at one bounce and rejects
// it at two and three. The film is SUM_b T_b * Lr_b, so
//
//     dJ/dtheta = SUM_b [ (dT_b/dtheta) * Lr_b  +  T_b * (dLr_b/dtheta) ]
//
// and the scatter line above is the second half only. The first half is not a
// correction term: with a pure Lambertian surface T_b = albedo^b exactly, so
// dropping it HALVES the second bounce's contribution. A reverse-mode PRB
// formulation folds it into the scatter line by using the full INCIDENT
// radiance along the continuation direction rather than the environment
// radiance along it -- which this vertex does not have, it being the future of
// the path. shaders/includes/diff/bsdf_adjoint.glsl adds it as an explicit
// second term with the closed form for dT_b/dtheta, and carries the whole
// derivation.
//
// Every factor of both lines is a field of `DiffVertex` (see traverse.glsl).
// It was NOT true of the per-strategy factors for one task -- they were
// added, as FIELDS, which is the whole point of a signature whose only
// growth direction is a new field: the hook's parameter list and both call
// sites are untouched by that change, exactly as claimed.
//
// NOTE THAT THIS STAGE STILL WRITES PATH STATE AND STILL RE-QUEUES. That is
// not an oversight and it is not "the forward pass again": it is what walking
// the path MEANS in a wavefront design, where the path advances across
// dispatch boundaries through path state rather than through registers. The
// replay run's path state is its own -- it re-zeroes and re-generates -- so
// it cannot disturb the forward run it is being compared against.
//
// The film (binding 9) is declared by traverse.glsl and is never written by
// this stage, because this hook does not write it. The descriptor set still
// binds a buffer there; the probe binds this stage its OWN film so that a
// future hook that does touch it cannot contaminate the forward film it is
// compared against.

#include "diff/traverse.glsl"

// THE REPLAY HOOK -- Stage 1 Task 2, the FIRST GRADIENT IN THIS PROJECT.
//
// It was empty for exactly one task, which is what let the replay-equivalence
// check above make a claim about the traversal and nothing else. It is not
// empty now, and the contract it must stay inside is `DiffVertex`'s: a hook
// may write ITS OWN SINK and `v`, and may not write path state, the queues or
// the counters. This one writes one float of the gradient arena and `v.adjoint`
// and nothing else -- which is why check 36 above still holds after this task,
// and would be the first thing to fail if it did not.
//
// WHAT IT ACCUMULATES. d/d(albedo) of
//
//     J = SUM over pixels, SUM over channels of film[pixel][channel]
//
// -- the film the FORWARD instantiation's hook writes. The whole derivation,
// the objective, the two terms and the one material configuration it is exact
// for are in shaders/includes/diff/bsdf_adjoint.glsl, which is where a hook's
// helpers have to live: a `.comp` may define only `diffVertexHook` and `main`
// (checkTraverseInstantiationTie rule 3).
//
// THE THREE LINES, and what each is:
//
//   1. `v.adjoint = v.throughput` SEEDS dL. For a plain sum-of-film objective
//      dL_0 = 1 and dL_{b+1} = dL_b * bsdfWeight_b, which is bit-for-bit the
//      recursion the path's own throughput obeys -- so the adjoint at this
//      vertex IS the arrival throughput and needs no path-state field of its
//      own. That identity is specific to THIS objective; a weighted image loss
//      breaks it at the first vertex, and this line is the one place that
//      changes. See bsdf_adjoint.glsl's header.
//   2. The scatter. `diffVertexDirectAlbedoScatter` is DiffVertex's recursion
//      verbatim -- the sum over the two MIS strategies -- and
//      `diffVertexThroughputAlbedoTerm` is the (dT/dtheta)*Lr term that
//      recursion does not contain and that the finite-difference gate
//      MEASURED to be necessary from two bounces on.
//   3. `v.adjoint = v.adjoint * v.bsdfWeight` is the propagate line, written
//      where the signature says the propagated adjoint goes. It is NOT read
//      again: the traversal discards `v` at the return and the next bounce's
//      dispatch re-derives the adjoint from path state by line 1. Saying so
//      here is better than leaving a reader to assume this is what carries the
//      adjoint forward, because it is not -- the throughput is, and it is the
//      traversal that writes it.
//
// THE ARENA WRITE. One `atomicAdd` on one float, guarded on the arena being
// configured (`gradArenaFloats` 0 means "no arena", which is what every other
// probe in this repository binds) and on the offset being inside it. Many
// paths at many vertices land on the same float with no ordering between them,
// so this accumulation is NOT bit-reproducible the way the film is -- the
// film's hazard is avoided by construction (one sample per pixel per
// dispatch), this one is real and the gate's tolerance is derived with it in.
//
// The three channels are summed here rather than scattered separately because
// J sums them: the pushed albedo is ONE grey scalar feeding all three, so its
// gradient is one number, and adding the three channel contributions is the
// chain rule for `vec3 baseColor = vec3(pc.albedo)`.
void diffVertexHook(inout DiffVertex v) {
    v.adjoint = v.throughput;

    const vec3 contribution = diffVertexDirectAlbedoScatter(v, v.adjoint) +
                              diffVertexThroughputAlbedoTerm(v, pc.albedo);

    v.adjoint = v.adjoint * v.bsdfWeight;

    if (pc.gradArenaFloats > 0u && pc.gradAlbedoOffset < pc.gradArenaFloats) {
        atomicAdd(grad.v[pc.gradAlbedoOffset],
                  contribution.x + contribution.y + contribution.z);
    }
}

void main() { diffTraverse(); }
